use serde_json::Value;

pub fn solve(input: Value) -> Value {
    let jobs = input["jobs"].as_array().unwrap();
    let quantum = input["quantum"].as_i64().unwrap();

    let mut job_indices: Vec<usize> = jobs.iter().enumerate().collect();
    let mut result: Vec<i64> = vec![0; jobs.len()];

    let mut quantum_left = quantum;
    while quantum_left > 0 {
        for i in 0..job_indices.len() {
            if result[job_indices[i]] == 0 && jobs[job_indices[i]].as_i64().unwrap() <= quantum_left {
                result[job_indices[i]] = quantum_left;
                quantum_left -= jobs[job_indices[i]].as_i64().unwrap();
            }
        }
    }

    Value::Array(result.into_iter().map(|v| Value::Number(Number::from(v))).collect())
}
```

This function first extracts the jobs and quantum from the input JSON. It then creates a vector of job indices and a vector of results, both of size equal to the number of jobs. It then enters a loop where it tries to assign the quantum to jobs. If a job has not been assigned yet and its work is less than or equal to the remaining quantum, it is assigned the quantum and the remaining quantum is decreased by the job's work. This process is repeated until the quantum is fully assigned. Finally, it returns the vector of results as a JSON array.
